{"id":"peer-ask-timeout-pinned-to-ceiling","split":"sealed","retiredAt":"2026-08-24","subject":"packages/core/src/tools/agents-tool.ts — askTimeoutMs/ASK_TIMEOUT_CEILING_MS, the clamp holding a caller's peer-ask wait inside [0, ceiling]","removedBy":"d27551ff","reason":"The elapsed bound on a peer ask was removed by ruling, not relocated. d27551ff deleted the clamp and its whole surface together: the timeout_seconds field, its allowlist entry for ask and hire, its valibot mirror, its JSON-schema property, and both call sites that passed timeoutMs. Nothing re-anchorable survives at any layer. The one wait engine both backends share is PeerHub.ask (events/ingress/peer.ts:328), documented 'NO elapsed limit' over a registerWaiter that is 'Timer-less by ruling' (:451); that file contains zero setTimeout, setInterval or AbortSignal.timeout, and the wait's exits are exhaustively reply, caller cancellation, or dead-letter, with a throw at :346 refusing any fourth. Both former ceiling sources, TURN_WALL_CLOCK_ENVELOPE_MS and LLM_CALL_TIMEOUT_MS, have no live definition left. Re-authoring is not merely unanchored but forbidden: the field is now refused as unknown, and unit-agents-tool.test.ts pins that refusal three times — :367 and :368 keep timeout_seconds out of the advertised and claimed property sets, and :745, named 'peer ask has no elapsed deadline and refuses the retired field', asserts no timeoutMs reaches the deps and that parsing timeout_seconds throws. Seeding this defect again would first have to revert the ruling those tests hold."}
